# Line endings and binary-ness, decided by git rather than by whichever editor
# or platform last touched a file. The companion to `.editorconfig`: that one
# tells an editor what to write, and this one makes git normalize what it
# stores, which is the half an editor's settings cannot reach.
#
# Why it earns its place: this package is a Linux library (libei is) that is
# also edited from Windows, where a global `core.autocrlf` would otherwise
# check these files out with CRLF -- and a shell script with CRLF endings
# fails on Linux with a confusing "bad interpreter" error. Every tracked file
# already has LF in the index -- measured, not assumed -- so there is nothing
# to renormalize for this to take effect.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` is what it puts in the
# working tree even on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text at all: git must never translate these.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf binary
*.so binary
*.pyc binary
*.whl binary
*.zip binary
*.tar.gz binary
